{"type":"result","result":"Now I have sufficient context to compile the findings. Let me structure my analysis and produce the final JSON report.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Hook matcher \\\"Bash\\\" is too broad, will fire on every Bash tool call\",\n      \"category\": \"performance\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \".claude/settings.json\",\n      \"line\": 18,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"\\\"matcher\\\": \\\"Bash\\\",\",\n      \"why\": \"The PostToolUse hook uses matcher \\\"Bash\\\" which triggers on EVERY Bash invocation in the session, not just gh pr create. This means the hook will execute and parse JSON input on every git command, auggie CLI invocation, find, grep, jq, etc. — potentially hundreds of times per session. The hook does fail-fast with exit 0 when the command doesn't match the regex, but the JSON parsing (jq calls on lines 20, 23, 34, 39) still executes unnecessarily. With a 3-second timeout, if the hook hangs on any Bash call, it introduces latency to unrelated commands.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add a pre-filter in the matcher to scope to gh commands only. Change to: \\\"matcher\\\": \\\"Bash(gh *)\\\". Confirm this pattern is supported (similar to how PreToolUse uses \\\"Write|Edit\\\"), or alternatively implement a faster early-exit check before the jq parsing (e.g., grep the INPUT string for 'gh pr create' before running jq).\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/auggie-flag-clear.sh:21\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Hook script does not validate or sanitize PR_NUM before interpolating into command suggestion\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh\",\n      \"line\": 47,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"INVOKE_HINT=\\\"/sc:auggie-review $PR_NUM\\\"\",\n      \"why\": \"PR_NUM is extracted from tool stdout via grep without validation (line 41: `grep -oE '[0-9]+$'`). If the regex fails to match or the URL is malformed, PR_NUM could be empty or contain unexpected characters. While the current extraction is robust against injection (grep -oE '[0-9]+$' limits to digits), the script does not verify PR_NUM is non-empty before interpolation. An empty PR_NUM results in the offer message suggesting '/sc:auggie-review ' with a trailing space, which is confusing and may cause the skill to misparse the invocation.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add validation after line 41: `[ -z \\\"$PR_NUM\\\" ] && PR_NUM=\\\"<PR-number>\\\"` to ensure the fallback case is used when extraction fails. This ensures the offer message is always well-formed.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh:41\", \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh:53\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Hook script jq invocations lack fallback when jq is not installed\",\n      \"category\": \"error-handling\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh\",\n      \"line\": 20,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"TOOL_NAME=\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\"$INPUT\\\" | jq -r '.tool_name // empty' 2>/dev/null)\\\"\",\n      \"why\": \"The hook uses jq for JSON parsing (lines 20, 23, 34, 39) with stderr redirected to /dev/null. If jq is not on PATH or fails, the variable assignments silently produce empty strings and the hook exits 0 early. This is fail-open by design (documented in comment line 2), but unlike other hooks (e.g., freshness-post-read.sh, auggie-flag-clear.sh) which use `|| echo \\\"fallback\\\"` patterns, this hook does not log the jq failure. If jq is missing, the hook silently never fires and the user never sees the review offer — no indication in logs that the hook attempted to run.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add a lightweight jq-availability check early (e.g., `command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0`) and optionally log a one-time diagnostic to ~/.claude/logs/offer-pr-review.log when jq is unavailable. Alternatively, document the jq dependency in the hook script header comment.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/freshness-post-read.sh:12\", \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/auggie-flag-clear.sh:14\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Regex for gh pr create detection does not escape pipe and semicolon correctly for all shells\",\n      \"category\": \"correctness\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh\",\n      \"line\": 29,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"if ! [[ \\\"$CMD\\\" =~ (^|[[:space:]]\\\\;\\\\&\\\\|])gh([[:space:]]+-[^[:space:]]+)*[[:space:]]+pr[[:space:]]+create([[:space:]]|$) ]]; then\",\n      \"why\": \"The regex uses `[[:space:]]\\\\;\\\\&\\\\|]` to match shell separators before `gh`. The intent is to match gh at word boundaries (start of command, or after space/semicolon/ampersand/pipe). However, the backslash escaping is inconsistent: `\\\\;\\\\&\\\\|` escapes semicolon and ampersand (which are not regex metacharacters in this context and don't need escaping), but the pipe `|` is the alternation metacharacter and should be `\\\\|` inside a bracket expression. In bash [[ ]] regex context, the pipe inside [...] is treated literally, so this works, but the inconsistent escaping is misleading and fragile if ported to POSIX sh or other regex engines.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Change to `(^|[[:space:];|&])gh` and rely on bracket expression literal matching. Alternatively, use `(^|[[:space:];\\\\&|])` with explicit escaping for all separators for clarity. Add a comment explaining the word-boundary logic.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [],\n      \"confidence\": \"low\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Missing session_id validation before interpolating into XML tag\",\n      \"category\": \"security\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh\",\n      \"line\": 57,\n      \"line_range_end\": 68,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"cat <<EOF\\n<sc-auggie-review-offer source=\\\"post-pr-create-hook\\\">\\nA PR was just created ($TARGET_HINT). Offer the user the option to run an Auggie-powered code review on it before continuing other work.\\n...\\n</sc-auggie-review-offer>\\nEOF\",\n      \"why\": \"The hook emits XML tags to stdout that Claude Code parses as structured output. The $TARGET_HINT variable (lines 46, 49, 52) is interpolated into the XML body without escaping. If PR_URL or TARGET_HINT contains XML metacharacters (e.g., a malicious PR URL like `https://github.com/foo/bar/pull/123?x=<script>`), the XML could be malformed. While GitHub sanitizes PR URLs, the hook does not validate that assumption. This is defense-in-depth: if the upstream gh pr create output is ever spoofed or the tool_response.stdout field is tampered with, the XML injection could break Claude Code's parsing.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Sanitize $TARGET_HINT before interpolation by replacing XML metacharacters: `TARGET_HINT=\\\"${TARGET_HINT//&/&amp;}\\\"` and similar for < > \\\" '. Alternatively, wrap the dynamic content in CDATA: `<![CDATA[$TARGET_HINT]]>`. This is a low-priority hardening measure since the attack surface requires spoofing gh CLI output.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [],\n      \"confidence\": \"low\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"SKILL.md documents incorrect sed pipeline for extracting Auggie JSON\",\n      \"category\": \"correctness\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/skills/sc-auggie-review-protocol/SKILL.md\",\n      \"line\": 166,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"Recommended: `sed -n '/^```json$/,/^```$/p' auggie-raw.json | sed '1d;$d' | jq '.'` or equivalent.\",\n      \"why\": \"The documented pipeline in SKILL.md line 166 is incomplete and will fail when --max-turns is passed. The skill's own \\\"Common pitfalls\\\" block (line 167) states that Auggie prints a preamble line `Applying --max-turns override:` before the JSON envelope when --max-turns is used, and this must be stripped with `tail -n +2`. However, the sed pipeline example on line 166 does not include this step. A reader following the line 166 example will encounter jq parse failures when --max-turns is present. The correct pipeline (verified in .dev/eval-workspaces/sc-auggie-review/iteration-3/VERIFICATION.md) is: `tail -n +2 auggie-raw.json | jq -r '.result' | sed -n '/^```json$/,/^```$/p' | sed '1d;$d' | jq .`\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Update line 166 to match the documented pitfall. Change to: `Recommended: tail -n +2 <file> | jq -r '.result' | sed -n '/^```json$/,/^```$/p' | sed '1d;$d' | jq '.'`. Alternatively, add a note that the pipeline assumes no --max-turns preamble and refer to line 167 for the full version.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/skills/sc-auggie-review-protocol/SKILL.md:167\", \".dev/eval-workspaces/sc-auggie-review/iteration-3/VERIFICATION.md:37\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"install_hooks.py registry entry for offer-pr-review.sh is project-local but script is not copied to .claude/hooks/\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/cli/install_hooks.py\",\n      \"line\": 179,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"\\\"offer-pr-review.sh\\\",\",\n      \"why\": \"The PR adds offer-pr-review.sh to _FRESHNESS_SCRIPTS (line 179), which causes `superclaude install` to copy it to ~/.claude/hooks/ on end-user machines. However, the .claude/settings.json registration (diff line 17) uses $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/offer-pr-review.sh, meaning the hook is project-local and only runs in this repo. End-user installs will get a copy of the script at ~/.claude/hooks/offer-pr-review.sh that is never registered and never runs. This wastes ~1KB per install and creates confusion: the script exists but is dormant. The comment on lines 171-179 says 'harmless when unregistered in unrelated projects', but the copy step is not harmless — it implies the script is intended to be registered globally, which contradicts the project-local design.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Remove offer-pr-review.sh from _FRESHNESS_SCRIPTS. Add a new constant _PROJECT_LOCAL_SCRIPTS for scripts that are not installed globally, and update the `=== Installer Registration ===` Makefile check to exclude project-local scripts from the drift detection. Alternatively, change the script to be globally-useful (e.g., make the offer conditional on a per-project opt-in flag).\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/install_hooks.py:66\", \"Makefile:308\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"No test coverage for the offer-pr-review.sh hook script logic\",\n      \"category\": \"tests\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/hooks/scripts/offer-pr-review.sh\",\n      \"line\": 1,\n      \"line_range_end\": 71,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# PostToolUse(Bash) — after a successful `gh pr create`, surface an offer to run /sc:auggie-review.\",\n      \"why\": \"The hook script contains non-trivial logic: JSON parsing (jq), regex matching for gh pr create, conditional extraction of PR URL/number, and XML generation. The PR adds no test file for this script. Other hooks in the repo (e.g., freshness-*.sh, auggie-flag-clear.sh) are tested via src/superclaude/cli/eval/hook_adapter.py integration tests. The offer-pr-review.sh script is not covered by that infrastructure because it's project-local, not part of the global hook bundle. If the regex on line 29 or the jq parsing logic on lines 20-41 breaks, there's no automated way to detect it before it ships.\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"Add a test file tests/hooks/test_offer_pr_review.sh or tests/hooks/test_offer_pr_review.py that: (1) feeds synthetic hook payloads (matching and non-matching Bash commands) to the script via stdin, (2) verifies the script exits 0 in all cases, (3) verifies the XML offer message is emitted only for gh pr create successes, (4) verifies PR_NUM and PR_URL extraction handles edge cases (malformed URLs, missing PR numbers, etc.). Use the hook_adapter.py testing pattern as a reference.\",\n      \"cross_references\": [\"src/superclaude/cli/eval/hook_adapter.py:1\", \"tests/skills/test_task_builder_merge.py:1\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"evals.json contains only prompts, no assertions or expected_output validation\",\n      \"category\": \"tests\",\n      \"severity_hint\": \"high\",\n      \"file\": \"src/superclaude/skills/sc-auggie-review-protocol/evals/evals.json\",\n      \"line\": 10,\n      \"line_range_end\": null,\n      \"in_diff\": true,\n      \"evidence\": \"\\\"assertions\\\": []\",\n      \"why\": \"The evals.json file defines 3 eval scenarios but every scenario has `assertions: []`. The expected_output field contains prose descriptions (e.g., 'A REVIEW.md at /tmp/eval-pr62/ that follows the skill's report template'), but there's no programmatic assertion logic to verify those outputs actually exist or match the schema. This means the evals are manual smoke tests, not automated regression tests. If the skill's output format drifts (e.g., REVIEW.md structure changes, file:line citations become hallucinated), the evals will pass silently. Cross-reference: the PRD skill (src/superclaude/skills/prd/) h